a664 81
*    7) Parsing of pixel list headers is considerably more complicated than
*       that of image headers since a pixel list may contain multiple images.
*       Moreover, the pixel list convention does not specify how to identify
*       the columns containing pixel coordinates (as opposed to pixel values
*       or ancillary data), nor how to associate columns with image axes.
*
*       However, the WCS keywords can help at least in part.  Pixel coordinate
*       columns may be identifed simply by the presence of a WCS keyword for
*       that column.  They can also to be "tied" into a single image via WCS
*       keywords and/or keyvalues; specifically via TPn_ka or TCn_ka keywords,
*       or via TCTYna keyvalues as a celestial axis pair.  For example, the
*       presence of TP11_12 in a header identifies columns 11 and 22 as
*       containing pixel coordinates and directly ties them together.  If
*       TP12_22 was also present then the combination of the two TPn_ka
*       keywords would indirectly tie columns 11 and 22.  Therefore, these
*       three columns must form part of a single image.
*
*       Where the header contains a single celestial axis pair, e.g. with
*       TCTYna keyvalues 'RA---SIN' and 'DEC--SIN', then the tie is obvious.
*       If there are multiple celestial axis pairs and they are
*       distinguishable, e.g. with TCTYna keyvalues 'RA---SIN' and 'DEC--SIN'
*       on the one hand and 'GLON-ZEA' and 'GLAT-ZEA' on the other, then the
*       ties are also obvious.  However, if they are indistinguishable, e.g.
*       two pairs of 'RA---ZPN'/'DEC--ZPN' then it may not be so obvious how
*       to pair them.  wcsbth() associates the longitude and latitude columns
*
*         1: firstly by any TPn_ka or TCn_ka keywords that may connect them,
*
*         2: if that fails, then by proximity, i.e. by having consecutive
*            column numbers.
*
*       If an association cannot be deduced as above, then all of the columns
*       are considered to be part of one image, but wcsbth() returns a warning
*       status because wcsset() cannot handle multiple celestial pairs and
*       would return an error status for the resulting wcsprm struct.  The
*       celestial axis pairs would have to be separated (based on a priori
*       knowledge) using wcssub().
*
*       In similar vein, two columns may be "anti-tied" if they have an
*       auxiliary WCS keyword of the same form with the same alternate "a"
*       but with differing keyvalue, i.e.
*
*            DAVGn  EQUIna LATPna LONPna MJDAn  MJDOBn OBSGXn OBSGYn
*            OBSGZn RADEna RFRQna RWAVna SOBSna SPECna SSRCna TWCSna
*            VANGna VSYSna ZSOUna
*
*       These columns cannot form part of a single image because the WCS
*       standard (and the WCSLIB implementation) does not provide for more
*       than one of these keywords per image.  Likewise, the standard does
*       not provide for multiple celestial axis pairs or spectral axes in a
*       single image.  If multiples occur in one pixel list header then the
*       related columns are also anti-tied.
*
*       wcsbth() returns an error status if it finds two columns that are both
*       tied and anti-tied.
*
*       Finally there may be columns that are neither tied nor anti-tied.
*       In order to avoid de facto inheritance of auxiliary keyvalues by
*       association in the wcsprm struct, those columns with an auxiliary WCS
*       keyword are considered to form a one-dimensional image.  The remainder
*       are all collected into a single image.
*
*       These rules are best illustrated by example.  Consider a header
*       containing the following keywords (among others):
*
*         TP1_2, RFRQ1, TWCS1,
*         RFRQ7,
*         TWCS16, TP15_16, CTYP16='RA---SIN', CTYP17='DEC--SIN'
*         CTYP21='RA---SIN', CTYP22='DEC--SIN'
*
*       Separate images are defined by columns [1,2], [7], [15,16,17] and
*       [21,22].  Columns 1 and 2 are tied via TP1_2.  If RFRQ1 and RFRQ7 have
*       differing keyvalues then columns 1 and 7 are anti-tied; it would be an
*       error for TP2_7 also to be present in this header.  Columns 16 and 17
*       are tied via the CTYP16 and CTYP17 keyvalues and in turn column 15 is
*       tied them via TP15_16.  It would also be an error for column 1 to be
*       tied to either column 15, 16, or 17 unless TWCS1 and TWCS16 had the
*       same keyvalue.  The celestial axes in columns 21 and 22 are considered
*       to be paired by proximity and form a separate image because the others
*       all have an auxiliary keyword.
*
@


